Package: OligoMetProfiler
Type: Package
Title: Theoretical Metabolite Libraries, Charge Envelopes, and MS/MS
    Fragment Ions for Therapeutic Oligonucleotides
Version: 1.0.0
Date: 2026-08-22
Authors@R: person("Nishikant", "Wase",
    email = "nishikant.wase@gmail.com",
    role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
    comment = "Author and developer")
Description: A general-purpose pipeline for in-silico metabolite
    identification of therapeutic oligonucleotides (antisense
    oligonucleotides, siRNA, and related modalities). This pipeline generates
    theoretical metabolite libraries (3'/5' exonuclease truncations and
    endonuclease internal fragments), molecular formulas, monoisotopic
    and average masses, negative-ESI charge envelopes with adduct
    variants, isotope patterns, phosphorothioate oxidation series,
    MS/MS fragment ions, and PRM inclusion lists for any
    sequence built from DNA, RNA, 2'-O-methyl, 2'-fluoro, MOE, cEt, or
    LNA chemistry with mixed PO/PS backbones. Here four approved oligonucleotide
    therapeutics (nusinersen, inotersen, patisiran, givosiran) are provided as working 
    examples spanning the major modality classes; two double as
    the formula engine's regression anchors, reproducing their
    published molecular formulas. Matching the
    theoretical library against uploaded mzML/mzXML or peak-list MS
    data (still under production).  This app exports a multi-sheet Excel workbook and HTML/PDF report, and
    writes targeted mass lists formatted for the Thermo Orbitrap
    Exploris Method Editor. Exports theoretical MS1 and MS2 spectral
    libraries as MGF and MSP. A Shiny dashboard (launched with
    run_app(), with the user guides built in) and command-line drivers
    wrap the same engine. FOR RESEARCH USE ONLY: not for diagnostic,
    clinical, or regulatory submission use. All outputs are computed
    predictions, not measurements, and must be confirmed experimentally;
    the software is provided without warranty and the author accepts no
    liability for its use (see DISCLAIMER.md).
